                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fb60b025-3291-395a-844f-5c39cc4e92d4",
                        "entry_id": "a847a94c-63e7-3d04-8670-4589f425cd42",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The breeding of an individual with a phenotypically recessive individual, in order to determine the zygosity of the former by analysing proportions of offspring phenotypes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "90fcbd7e-3ccc-4fe7-8e08-9a6282d7f795",
                                "sense_id": "fb60b025-3291-395a-844f-5c39cc4e92d4",
                                "text": "The inbreds of groups 3 and 4 have a distinct difference in plant height, except for a few selections, presumably because of some difference in selection pressure at low- and high-stand densities. Group means for testcross performance showed more progress in group 4 than in group 3 because of a difference in the number of low-yielding genotypes in the two groups."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8712c00c-7f66-4158-963c-fb242d68bc0b",
                                "sense_id": "fb60b025-3291-395a-844f-5c39cc4e92d4",
                                "text": "You could determine its genotype by performing a testcross. If the plant were homozygous (TT), a testcross would produce all tall progeny (TT × tt → all Tt); if the plant were heterozygous (Tt), half of the progeny would be tall and half would be short (Tt × tt → 1/2 Tt and 1/2 tt). When a testcross is performed, any recessive allele in the unknown genotype is expressed in the progeny, because it will be paired with a recessive allele from the homozygous recessive parent."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
